Subject: [PATCH] feat: worker blueprints and session lifecycle fixes

X-Jcode-Patch-Intent: enable reusable worker blueprints with coordinator policy, one trusted allowlisted worker-to-child spawn edge, trusted worker hook identity, and blueprint-scoped resume of finished workers
X-Jcode-Patch-Kind: personal-feature
X-Jcode-Patch-Depends-On: 0002-candidate-add-hook-context-and-post-tool-transform.patch, 1001-personal-provider-openrouter-eu-and-auth-provider-selection.patch
X-Jcode-Patch-File: 1003-personal-feature-worker-blueprints-and-session-lifecycle-fixes.patch

Patch intent:
Add a worker blueprint system that lets a jcode user define reusable,
specialized subagent roles in Markdown. Each blueprint supplies durable
role instructions, model, effort, enabled-tools, disabled-tools,
communication-policy, and timeout. At runtime, every valid blueprint
generates a coordinator-visible tool named swarm_<id> that spawns a
blueprint-backed worker, waits for completion, and returns a compact
summary with paginated follow-up reads. Workers submit that completion report as
their final assistant response, never through an unavailable swarm report tool.
A reserved coordinator.md file
supplies the coordinator's durable prompt and tool policy
(enabled-tools/disabled-tools), configured via the shared
[agents.worker_blueprints.coordinator] override namespace. The old
allowed-tools key is replaced by enabled-tools and disabled-tools,
matching the global [tools] policy vocabulary.

The selected blueprint name and enabled/disabled tool policy are persisted
in session snapshots, startup projections, journals, and forks. Agents own
the runtime policy registration for their lifetime. Visible and headless
spawn, session restoration, wake, and revival restore the saved policy before
worker execution, without expanding authority or restoring obsolete tools.

Swarms gain exactly one trusted worker edge beyond the root fan-out, and
its policy surface is fully generic: a depth-1 worker may spawn one
further generation of any blueprint named in its allowed-child-
blueprints list (the child lands at depth 2 and can itself never spawn,
whatever its own list contains). The allowlist comes from the blueprint
front-matter `allowed-child-blueprints` key, replaced wholesale by the
TOML `agents.worker_blueprints.<id>.allowed_child_blueprints` override
when present (an explicit empty override clears it), and it is persisted
with the session's `WorkerBlueprintPolicy` so it is the trusted identity
for spawn admission, read admission, and spawn-tool visibility. No
native tier name appears in any policy decision: admission checks the
persisted allowlist only, so arbitrary blueprint names such as
reviewer -> summarizer work end to end.

The requester's identity is its persisted worker blueprint policy
(server-set at spawn time and never model-editable), not the report-back
chain or tool visibility: a visible worker carries no parent id and
member edges go stale across reloads. The same persisted policy drives
hook metadata, so real generated-tool workers report worker role, worker
id, and blueprint id/name in both headless and visible modes, while
generic spawned workers keep the parent-id fallback and roots stay
coordinators.

The edge stays usable end to end: the spawned child's completed report
must reach its non-coordinator parent through the real generated
`swarm_<id>` tool path (CommSpawn, Await, then the full-context Read
that fetches the report, with deterministic follow-up paging over the
same read). Full-context read admission grants exactly the trusted
immediate parent of a child whose blueprint its persisted allowlist
names: the target's server-set, persisted `report_back_to_session_id`
edge names the requester, and the target's persisted blueprint name is
in the requester's persisted allowed-child-blueprints list. Same-swarm
membership stays a prerequisite, the coordinator and target-session
grants are unchanged, and no other shape is granted: siblings, reverse
reads (a child reading its parent), deeper generations, workers with no
matching allowlist entry, custom or missing identities, and non-allowed
targets keep the denial, so a report-back edge or an unverified client
claim alone never grants full context. The read denial message states
this scope truthfully.

The child must run on the concrete effective model configured for its
blueprint (front matter merged with TOML overrides): an inherit sentinel
or a missing model is rejected, and worker-originated model overrides
are rejected outright. Admission failures are immediate errors;
capacity caps keep their existing immediate, non-queued behavior. A
spawn whose await times out reports the timeout and leaves the child
running for follow-up instead of stopping it.

The generated tool gains a third input mode: `resume_session` (a session
ID the tool previously returned) with a `task` continues that finished
worker on its existing session instead of spawning a replacement. The
resumed worker keeps its original working directory, model, and
persisted blueprint policy: revival runs a second turn on the same
session with no second spawn, and the persisted `WorkerBlueprintPolicy`
(model, tools, child allowlist) survives the reload and the new turn.
Conflicting inputs are rejected before any RPC: resume cannot combine
with `follow_up_session`, `working_dir`, or `spawn_mode`, a blank
`resume_session` falls back to spawn mode, and a worker in an active
status is rejected before any control request so a busy worker is
never interrupted.

The resume request is scoped to the tool's own blueprint on the native
`comm_task_control` wire: the generated tool sends an optional
`blueprint` field, and the server checks the target's persisted
blueprint name from server-owned state right after assignee validation,
before any plan mutation, dispatch, requeue, reassignment, interrupt,
or live turn, and again in the wake path before availability and
revival, so a resume can never act on another blueprint's worker and
the generic `swarm` tool (which omits the field) keeps its current
behavior. Scoped authorization mirrors the full-context read rule:
only the coordinator or the trusted immediate parent, proven by the
persisted report-back edge and a persisted allowlist naming the
target's blueprint, may resume; non-coordinator ancestors are rejected
for scoped requests even though generic wake stays allowed, and every
identity resolves from the persisted policy (resident agent or session
snapshot), never from client-editable request state.

A scoped resume never queues. When the target is not idle the server
answers `busy` atomically, without queueing a soft interrupt or
touching mutable owner state, and the tool reports an honest
retry-later error. The tool captures a message-count watermark before
the new turn and succeeds only on a non-empty new assistant report
past that watermark: growth alone (wake messages, tool calls, empty
turns) re-arms the wait, a stale terminal status re-checks on a short
delay instead of trusting an old final, and the completed-but-no-output
success path is gone, so a resume that produces no report fails
honestly.

Worker spawn-tool visibility becomes strict and generic: a worker sees
only the generated `swarm_<id>` tools for its allowlisted child
blueprints; the generic `swarm` tool and any new or unknown spawn tool
are denied for every worker identity, classification stays by the
swarm-tool name class so future registered spawn tools are filtered
too, a disabled tool stays denied even when its id is allowlisted, and
validate_tool_allowed refuses a spawn tool the worker cannot see even
when named directly.

Schema and compatibility invariants: allowlist entries are trimmed,
must be valid blueprint IDs, duplicates collapse deterministically to
the first occurrence, self-references are rejected so no blueprint can
grant itself a child spawn, and references to blueprints that are not
registered fail closed at spawn admission without excluding the owner
blueprint or any unrelated blueprint (they are diagnosed at load). The
reserved coordinator override rejects the worker-only
`allowed_child_blueprints` field, and sessions persisted before the
field existed deserialize with no children.

Two session-lifecycle fixes ride with this series because both were
found by using blueprint workers. When a comm access check fails, the server
now distinguishes a target session that is no longer a swarm member
(it exited, crashed, or was stopped and was removed during cleanup)
from a genuine swarm mismatch, and from a requester that is not in any
swarm. A client that attached to an agent already living in the server
(a popped-out swarm worker window, or any resume attach to a live
session) detaches on disconnect without stopping the agent, the
session, or its swarm membership. The TUI signal path no longer
relabels sessions on termination signals; the server remains the sole
authority for session status on disconnect.

Why it exists:
Worker blueprints let a user specialize subagents without code changes.
A blueprint can pin a cheap model for routine work, restrict its tool
access, control its communication, and cap its runtime. The generated
tool returns a bounded summary (2000 chars) so the coordinator does not
burn tokens reading the worker's full output. A report over the limit is
shown as head and tail around an elision marker that names the omitted
char range and the follow-up offset for it, so conclusions at the end stay
visible. A deterministic follow-up read (no LLM) pages the same final
report by default, so the marker's offsets index exactly the omitted text
and never the worker prompt. scope "transcript" instead pages the whole
worker conversation newest-first (offset 0 is the end), and every page
states its scope and direction. The coordinator.md file lets a user define the
coordinator's own prompt and tool policy without installing content into
a global prompt overlay. The reserved coordinator ID uses the same
[agents.worker_blueprints.<id>] override namespace as ordinary workers,
accepting only enabled and disabled (not model, effort, or
timeout-minutes). When the swarm feature is off, swarm and generated
swarm_<id> tools are omitted from the session tool surface and rejected
at execution, the root coordinator has no coordinator-specific tool limit
or prompt applied, and swarm-specific agent settings are omitted from
the config summary. Runtime toggles remove and restore that coordinator
policy and prompt while preserving global tool limits.

Without the trusted worker edge only the root session could spawn, so a
delegated worker could never extract bounded child work even when its
blueprint promised it could, and hook metadata mislabeled visible
generated-tool workers as coordinators because it keyed on parent id
alone. Even with the spawn edge admitted, the parent still could not
collect its child's work: the generated tool's report fetch and
follow-up reads are full-context reads, which only the coordinator and
the target session could perform, so a non-coordinator parent got a
denial naming a nonexistent worktree-manager grant and the child's
completed report was unreachable. Trusting client-editable request state
or tool visibility for admission or read access would let a worker
escalate cost by spawning deeper generations, on an expensive parent
model, or by reading arbitrary swarm context, so identity, depth, the
child allowlist, model, and read parenthood all come from server-owned
state. Hardcoding tier names in the native policy would freeze the
catalog's worker graph into the binary; the allowlist keeps the same
trusted edge with the graph declared in blueprint data.

Both failures were observed live with spawned blueprint workers.
A worker removed from the swarm during cleanup made the coordinator's follow-up
read fail with a misleading "not in the same swarm" error, sending the
coordinator hunting for a swarm split that never happened. Separately,
closing the popped-out terminal window of a running inline worker
delivered SIGHUP to the viewer TUI, which marked the worker's session
Crashed from a stale client copy, and the disconnect cleanup then
force-stopped the agent and removed the swarm member, so the
coordinator's report fetch failed. Before the viewer attached, the
member existed without any client at all, so a viewer leaving must
restore exactly that state. Intentional non-goals: which reads are
allowed is unchanged and removed members are not restored; a
connection that drove its own unfinished turn keeps the existing
Crashed cleanup; idle unsaved empty-root sessions keep their
reconnect-grace behavior.

Spawn-only delegation forced a fresh worker (model boot, prompt, and
policy registration) whenever a finished worker's next task arrived,
and the only reuse path was the generic wake: any ancestor could drive
it, it queued a soft interrupt against a busy worker, and nothing tied
the request to the blueprint that owned the session. Resume reuses the
worker's warm session under the tool's own blueprint identity, with
the persisted policy, not the report-back chain or client claims,
deciding who may resume which worker. The watermark rule exists
because a resumed turn can end with history growth but no report;
without it the tool would surface a stale final as if it were the new
answer.

