Update guidance:
Preserve the documented ~/.jcode/worker-blueprints/ default, including
symlinked directories and Markdown files. The reserved coordinator ID
must never be accepted as an ordinary worker blueprint or generate a
swarm_coordinator tool. The coordinator.md front matter accepts only
enabled-tools and disabled-tools; no name, description, model, effort,
communication-policy, or timeout-minutes. The [agents.worker_blueprints.
coordinator] override accepts only enabled and disabled; worker-only
fields (model, effort, timeout_minutes) are rejected. Preserve the
precedence chain for model (per-spawn > blueprint > swarm_model >
coordinator) and timeout (per-call > blueprint > config > default 20m).
Generated swarm_<id> descriptions are user-owned and exempt from the
static built-in description token cap. Worker completion prompts must
request a final assistant response and must not require unavailable swarm
tools. Blueprint enabled-tools are
always combined with the global tool policy. Coordinator policy only
applies to root sessions, never to spawned workers or internal roles.
The coordinator.md body is injected as a stable static segment in the
root system prompt only when swarm is enabled. Communication-policy none
blocks ordinary messages but not tool results. Generated tools default
to headless execution. Generated tools never overwrite existing tool
names. All test SwarmMember and WorkerBlueprintOverride constructions
must include the new fields. Spawn summaries and report-scope follow-ups
must use the same final_report text so summary offsets stay valid.
Transcript scope must stay newest-first and label its direction. Protocol
validation must include provider-core tests to exercise upstream Tokio macro
feature unification.

Keep identity, depth, child allowlist, model, and read-parenthood
decisions server-owned: the persisted policy is the single trusted
source, and the admission and read gates must never consult tool
visibility or client-editable fields. Admission is by the persisted
allowed-child-blueprints list only; never reintroduce a native tier-name
check anywhere in the policy path. Preserve the swarm-tool name-class
filter so future generated spawn tools are covered without editing the
match, and keep disabled tools denied even when allowlisted. The
full-context parent grant must keep both grounds (persisted report-back
edge, requester allowlist naming the target's persisted blueprint) plus
the same-swarm prerequisite: dropping any of them widens swarm context
access. Keep rejecting self-references and invalid IDs at load, keep
unknown references failing closed at admission without excluding
unrelated blueprints, and keep the explicit empty TOML override winning
over a nonempty front-matter list. The depth bound (only depth-1 workers
spawn, children are terminal) must hold even when a child's own allowlist
is nonempty. Model override rejection and the concrete-model requirement
are deliberate cost controls; relax them only with an explicit policy
decision. Keep admission failures immediate (no queue, no reservation)
and preserve root capacity semantics.

Resume must stay scoped to the tool's own blueprint: the generated tool
sends its blueprint ID on the native comm_task_control request and the
server verifies the target's persisted blueprint name from
server-owned state before any plan mutation, revival, or dispatch;
never trust a client-supplied identity claim. A scoped resume that
finds the target busy must answer busy atomically (no soft interrupt,
no queue, no mutable owner state) while generic unscoped wake keeps
queueing. Keep the watermark rule: success requires a non-empty new
assistant report past the pre-resume message count, growth alone
re-arms, a stale terminal status must re-check instead of passing as
the new final, and the completed-without-output success path stays
removed. Revival must run the second turn on the same session with no
second spawn, keeping the persisted WorkerBlueprintPolicy across
reload and the new turn. Scoped requester authorization stays limited
to the coordinator and the trusted immediate parent with the same
grounds as the full-context read grant, and the generic swarm tool
keeps omitting the blueprint field so its behavior is unchanged.

The three comm access failure modes must stay distinct, with the
historical mismatch message preserved verbatim for the
both-members-present case. The viewer skip in disconnect cleanup must
stay after the successor-attached check and gated on the Closed
disposition, so viewer-owned turns keep crashing on disconnect, and
attached_to_live_agent must only be set when the claimed agent
pre-existed in the sessions map. If a future TUI mode hosts the agent
in-process, reintroduce session relabeling for that mode only.

